462890625, 0.0214691162109375, 0.004947662353515625, 0.0158233642578125, 0.001895904541015625, -0.01094818115234375, 0.0069122314453125, -0.0027370452880859375, -0.0231170654296875, -0.03118896484375, -0.004573822021484375, -0.0255279541015625, -0.043701171875, -0.0125732421875, -0.0008940696716308594, -0.004650115966796875, -0.001338958740234375, -0.0241546630859375, 0.0048980712890625, -0.0189971923828125, 0.044281005859375, 0.0260009765625, 0.0241851806640625, 0.007572174072265625, -0.03094482421875, 0.03875732421875, 0.047454833984375, 0.0152587890625, 0.0157928466796875, 0.00928497314453125, -0.04119873046875, -0.01641845703125, 0.00015926361083984375, 0.0036525726318359375, 0.0170745849609375, 0.0158233642578125, -0.0078125, -0.0131378173828125, -0.01532745361328125, -0.00019288063049316406, -0.0007824897766113281, -0.0343017578125, -0.0094451904296875, 0.0017070770263671875, -0.00972747802734375, -0.0262451171875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Democracy can be defined in various ways, with two prominent approaches being the minimalist and maximalist definitions. A minimalist definition of democracy refers to a system in which the primary criterion is the existence of regular, competitive elections that allow citizens to choose their leaders. In contrast, a maximalist definition includes not only these electoral procedures but also a broader set of requirements, such as the protection of civil liberties and human rights.\n\nJoseph Schumpeter, in his influential work \"Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy\" (1942), is often associated with the minimalist perspective. He argued that democracy is best understood as an institutional arrangement for arriving at political decisions in which individuals acquire the power to decide by means of a competitive struggle for the people\u2019s vote. On the other hand, Robert Dahl, particularly in \"Polyarchy\" (1971), advanced a maximalist view by emphasizing not only free and fair elections but also the necessity of freedoms like speech, assembly, and equal legal protection as essential components of a democratic system.\n\nEach definition has attracted criticism. The minimalist approach is often faulted for ignoring substantive elements of democracy, such as the actual protection of rights or the inclusiveness of participation. For example, a country might hold regular elections but suppress opposition voices or restrict media freedom, thus meeting the minimalist criteria while failing to uphold democratic values in practice. Conversely, maximalist definitions are sometimes criticized for being too broad or difficult to operationalize. Including a wide range of civil liberties and social rights can make it challenging to determine whether a country qualifies as a democracy, as the measurement of these rights can be subjective and vary across contexts.\n\nDebates over these definitions remain active among political scientists and policymakers. Key points of contention include whether democracy should be assessed solely by its procedures or also by its outcomes, and how to balance the need for clear, measurable criteria with the desire to capture the full range of democratic practices. Scholars such as Adam Przeworski and Larry Diamond have contributed to these debates, with differing views on the appropriate scope of democratic criteria.\n\nThe complexity and contested nature of the concept of democracy stem from disagreements over which features are essential and how they should be measured. This matters for practical evaluation because the choice of definition affects how countries are classified, how international organizations assess democratic progress, and how policy recommendations are formulated. Recognizing these differences is crucial for understanding the diversity of democratic practices worldwide and for making informed judgments about the quality and legitimacy of political systems. In real-world analysis and policy, being clear about which definition is used can influence everything from foreign aid decisions to the design of democracy promotion programs."}}